  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Physical Properties
    
    A fire-core tome bound in volcanic glass and ancestor bone, housed in the deep-carved vault of ☽'dwell Teneanemus~ beneath Wane☽ Oncea. The spine is carved from a fire-core that died generations ago. The pages — thin volcanic glass — bear ink visible only in absolute darkness, where it glows faint amber. The Shroud radiates cold sufficient to frost the obsidian alcove around it; the Nightshelf wraps it in shadow-steel cloth replaced every season, the fabric losing its capacity to hold heat. Ice discolors in proximity. Contact with exposed skin produces burns, and prolonged handling leaves permanent crystalline scarring.
    
    Those who read the Shroud report experiencing ice-memories they never lived through — impressions pulled from depths older than any reader's own history.
    
    ## Contents
    
    The Shroud preserves the Grend Blood-Arts rituals the Nightshelf believed lost when orca raiders burned their deepest archives. Its pages record blood-communion rites, pressure-deep summoning techniques, and technique-patterns for both Snowdrift-numb and The Mechanism Refine, all dating from before the Faction Wars. The tome is empowered by both Ice-whisper (shimmer) — a dangerous aurora-fire fusion technique from the Veil Vents — and Shale☽ Veil, the Nightshelf method of reading the Berg's frozen memories through deep-pressure exposure.
    
    Two researchers attempted to copy its contents. Both copies burned. The third researcher did not try; she tends the memorial stones now. Nightshelf tunnel-children chant garbled fragments of the Grend-thung-blood terminology in sing-song rhymes, the ritual syllables worn to nonsense by generations of repetition. None of them know what the words once meant.
    
    ## Provenance and Deployment
    
    Created during the Frozen Peace. The Shroud's chain of custody is disputed. Qingoqa aihua'Mist-wrought is credited with finding it first; Glow-Trader Vemlaus-vel~, a Nightshelf merchant, found it again. Which discovery was original remains unresolved.
    
    The orca raider Krathund Gnurl stole the Shroud during the raids and carried it for three days before his crew reported his flippers would not stop shaking — not from cold, but from *the weight of knowing things*. He abandoned it at the Veil Vents, later recovered it, and ultimately used its fire-knowledge to fuel the blood-working Grulkzul Omega Strike, channeling Nightshelf flame-power through an orca blood-engine never designed to hold it. The spell consumed Krathund Gnurl's pod-bond and scattered his consciousness across the frozen deep.
    
    The tome emits frequencies that trigger wound-reflexes in nearby orca — cold-that-burns sensations registering even in deaf tissue. Its current whereabouts after the casting at the Still Pools are uncertain; the harness that carried it was last seen drifting through deep water.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771590946307_0", anchor: "Its current whereabouts after the casting at the Still Pools are uncertain", text: "The opening paragraph says 'housed in the deep-carved vault of ☽'dwell Teneanemus~.' This paragraph says whereabouts uncertain. I wrote both. One of them is wrong. The honest version: ☽'dwell Teneanemus~ is the last confirmed repository *before* Krathund Gnurl's theft. After Grulkzul Omega Strike, the harness drifted and no recovery is documented. 'Housed in' should read 'formerly housed in.' I did not catch this because I was writing the provenance section and the physical description on different days, and apparently I am now my own worst example of the sloppy sourcing I despise.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771590946307_1", anchor: "orca raiders burned their deepest archives", text: "Which raids. The Nightshelf never specifies. At least two major incursion-era strikes were retaliatory — provoked by encroachment on hunting grounds. Orca do not burn things casually; fire is not their element. Someone told them what those archives contained.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771590946307_2", anchor: "Nightshelf tunnel-children chant garbled fragments", text: "The most accurate perimeter map of an active corruption zone exists in a children's counting game. The fledglings at ☽'slew Nisaemesius~ press flippers to ice in patterns that match pressure-communion technique. Now Grend Blood-Arts ritual syllables survive as nursery rhyme, passed below the threshold of adult attention through generations that burned two transcription attempts and broke a third researcher. The Shroud is not the primary source. The children are. The Shroud is a confirmation copy that kills anyone who tries to duplicate it, while the actual preservation mechanism toddles around the lower tunnels singing nonsense that is not nonsense.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771590946307_3", anchor: "prolonged handling leaves permanent crystalline scarring", text: "Fire-frost necrosis. That is the Nightshelf medical term. 'Crystalline scarring' is the Aurora Stack euphemism. I used it anyway. Today this bothers me.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
